# Task API

A simple in-memory to-do list CRUD API built with FastAPI. No database — data
lives in a Python list and resets whenever the server restarts.

## Setup

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## Run

```bash
uvicorn main:app --reload --port 8000
```

The API is now at `http://localhost:8000`.

## Swagger UI (Stage 5)

Interactive API docs, generated automatically by FastAPI:

- Swagger UI: http://localhost:8000/docs
- ReDoc: http://localhost:8000/redoc
- Raw OpenAPI schema: http://localhost:8000/openapi.json

## Endpoints

| Method | Path          | Description                          | Success | Errors             |
|--------|---------------|---------------------------------------|---------|---------------------|
| GET    | `/`           | API description                       | 200     | —                   |
| GET    | `/health`     | Health check                          | 200     | —                   |
| GET    | `/tasks`      | List all tasks                        | 200     | —                   |
| GET    | `/tasks/{id}` | Get one task                          | 200     | 404 if not found    |
| POST   | `/tasks`      | Create a task (`{"title": "..."}`)    | 201     | 400 if title missing/empty |
| PUT    | `/tasks/{id}` | Update a task (`{"title","done"}`)    | 200     | 404 unknown id, 400 invalid body |
| DELETE | `/tasks/{id}` | Delete a task                         | 204     | 404 if not found    |

## Checkpoints (curl)

### Stage 0 — Hello, server
```bash
curl -i http://localhost:8000/
```

### Stage 1 — Root & health
```bash
curl -i http://localhost:8000/
curl -i http://localhost:8000/health
```

### Stage 2 — Read
```bash
curl -i http://localhost:8000/tasks
curl -i http://localhost:8000/tasks/1     # 200 + one task
curl -i http://localhost:8000/tasks/99    # 404 + {"error": "Task 99 not found"}
```

### Stage 3 — Create
```bash
curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8000/tasks \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"title":"Buy milk"}'               # 201

curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8000/tasks \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"title":""}'                       # 400
```

### Stage 4 — Update & Delete (full lifecycle)
```bash
# Create
curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8000/tasks \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"title":"Test task"}'              # 201

# Update (assume id 4)
curl -i -X PUT http://localhost:8000/tasks/4 \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"title":"Test task - updated","done":false}'   # 200

# Mark done
curl -i -X PUT http://localhost:8000/tasks/4 \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"title":"Test task - updated","done":true}'    # 200

# Delete
curl -i -X DELETE http://localhost:8000/tasks/4       # 204

# Confirm
curl -i http://localhost:8000/tasks                   # 200, task 4 gone

# Errors
curl -i -X PUT http://localhost:8000/tasks/999 \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"title":"x","done":false}'         # 404
curl -i -X DELETE http://localhost:8000/tasks/999      # 404
```

## Notes

- Data is **in-memory only** — a restart resets it back to the 3 seed tasks.
- Validation errors (missing/empty `title`, wrong types, etc.) are caught by
  Pydantic and converted from FastAPI's default `422` into a `400` with a
  `{"error": "..."}` body, per the spec.
- All "not found" responses use a consistent `{"error": "Task <id> not found"}`
  shape across GET, PUT, and DELETE.

---

## Stage 7: AI vs me

### The prompt (v1 — placeholder, written to test the process)

> "Build a REST API in Python using FastAPI for a to-do list app. Don't use a
> database — store tasks in memory. Include endpoints to list all tasks, get
> a single task by id, create a new task, update a task, and delete a task.
> Use proper HTTP status codes for success and errors. Validate input and
> return an error if the task title is empty. Include Swagger UI docs. A
> task should have id, title, and done fields."

The AI's code is in `ai-version/main.py`. It started on the first try with
`uvicorn main:app --port 8001` — no errors, no missing dependencies.

My own code (`main.py`) also starts clean on `port 8005` in testing (an
earlier draft had a `TaskUpdate`/`TaskChange` name mismatch that crashed on
import — fixed before this comparison).

### Stage 4 checkpoint results

| Check | Expected | My code | AI's code (`ai-version/`) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Server starts | starts clean | ✅ | ✅ |
| `GET /tasks/1` | 200 | ✅ 200 | ✅ 200 |
| `GET /tasks/99` | 404 + `{"error": "Task 99 not found"}` | 404, but `{"detail": "Task 99 not found"}` (right code, wrong key, id included) | 404, but `{"detail": "Task not found"}` (right code, wrong key, **no id**) |
| `POST /tasks` valid title | 201 | ✅ 201 | ✅ 201 |
| `POST /tasks` empty title | 400 | ✅ 400 | ❌ 422 |
| `POST /tasks` missing title | 400 | ❌ 422 (no handling — falls straight to Pydantic) | ❌ 422 |
| `PUT /tasks/{id}` empty title | 400 | ✅ 400 | ❌ 422 |
| `PUT /tasks/{id}` unknown id | 404 | ✅ 404 | ✅ 404 |
| `PUT /tasks/{id}` partial update (done only) | 200 | ✅ 200 (supports partial via `Optional` fields) | ✅ 200 (also supports partial) |
| `DELETE /tasks/{id}` | 204, no body | ✅ 204, no body | ❌ 200 + message body |
| `DELETE` unknown id | 404 | ✅ 404 | ✅ 404 |
| `GET /docs` | 200 | ✅ 200 | ✅ 200 |

**Score: mine passes 9/11, the AI's passes 7/11.** The two shared misses
(404 body shape, missing-title-on-POST) both trace back to gaps in the
prompt/spec, not implementation quality.

Full diffs: `ai-version/diff-vs-hand-built.txt` (mine vs. AI v1),
generated with `diff --unified main.py ai-version/main.py`.

### What did the AI do better — and do I understand it?

- Honestly, not much on correctness — my code passes more of the actual
  checkpoints. The one place the AI's structure is slightly cleaner is
  that it defines `TaskUpdate` right where it's used with no separate
  "create" vs "update" naming inconsistency to trip over — a reminder that
  naming things consistently (`TaskCreate`/`TaskChange` vs.
  `TaskCreate`/`TaskUpdate`) matters more than it seems while writing code
  quickly.
- Both of us independently made `PUT`'s body fields `Optional`, so a
  partial update (e.g. just flipping `done`) works without resending the
  title. I understand exactly why this works (Pydantic `Optional[...] =
  None` fields simply get skipped in the update loop) and would make the
  same choice again.

### What did it get wrong or quietly ignore from my prompt?

1. **`POST`/`PUT` with an empty title returns 422, not 400.** It relied on
   Pydantic's default required-field validation instead of writing an
   explicit check, so FastAPI's default `422` won by default. My prompt
   said "return an error if the title is empty" without a code, and it
   filled that gap with the framework default — the same gap I filled
   correctly in my own code by writing an explicit `if not title: raise
   HTTPException(400, ...)` check.
2. **`DELETE` returns `200` + a message body, not `204` with an empty
   body.** I never specified 204-on-delete in the prompt; it picked the
   more tutorial-common "200 + confirmation message" pattern. My own code
   gets this right because I set `status_code=204` explicitly on the route
   decorator and returned nothing.
3. **404 error bodies never include the task id** (`"Task not found"` vs.
   my `"Task {id} not found"`), and use `{"detail": ...}` rather than a
   shape I might have wanted (`{"error": ...}`) — I never specified an
   error envelope at all, so both of us landed on FastAPI's
   `HTTPException` default, but only I happened to interpolate the id into
   the message.

### What did my prompt forget to specify — and what did the AI decide for me?

- **Exact status codes per case** — I said "use proper HTTP status codes,"
  not "422 must become 400" or "delete must be 204," so it silently chose
  Pydantic/FastAPI defaults for both. My own code only avoided this because
  I happened to write explicit checks while coding by hand — not because I
  specified it precisely to myself either. That's the real lesson: coding
  by hand forces decisions a vague prompt lets an AI skip.
- **Error response shape** — never specified an envelope
  (`{"error": ...}` vs `{"detail": ...}`), so `{"detail": ...}` won by
  default in both versions.
- **Seed data** — I never told the AI to start with sample tasks, so its
  list starts empty; my own code has 3 seed tasks because I decided that
  for myself while building, not because I specified it in the prompt.
- **Missing vs. empty title as separate validation cases** — neither my
  code nor the AI's actually returns 400 for a `title` key that's absent
  entirely (only for an empty string); both fall through to Pydantic's 422
  there. That's a genuine spec gap on my end, and it shows up identically
  in both implementations — a good sign that when I *do* specify a case
  clearly enough to code it myself, an AI given the same case will usually
  get it right too; when I don't, neither does.

### One rematch (v2)

**What changed in the prompt:** I added explicit status codes per case
(200/201/204/400/404 — including that a *missing* `title` field, not just
an empty string, must also return 400), required the error body shape
`{"error": "<message>"}` for every error case including the task id in
404 messages, specified 3 seed tasks at startup, and said `DELETE` must
return 204 with no response body.

Result (`ai-version-v2/main.py`): the previously-failing checks now pass —
empty *and* missing title both return `400` with `{"error": "..."}"`
instead of `422`/`{"detail":...}`, and `DELETE` returns `204` with no body
instead of `200` + a message. Being explicit about status codes and the
error envelope, instead of just saying "use proper status codes," was the
single biggest lever — the AI followed precise instructions accurately; it
just couldn't read my mind on the vague ones, the same way my own code
only got things right where I'd already made a firm decision myself before
writing a line of Python.